# Documentation

- Prefer descriptive names over comments (`getVehicleById`, not `getData`; `isDueForService`, not `check`).
- Comments explain WHY, never WHAT. Document workarounds, trade-offs, browser quirks, and ticket links.
- Every exported function, class, and type gets a JSDoc/TSDoc covering purpose, params, return value, and thrown errors or edge cases.
- Keep a README per feature module: what it does, how to use it, gotchas. Update it in the same change as the code.
- When adding or changing an API route, update request/response shape, status codes, and auth in the same change.
- Delete commented-out code. Version control is the archive.
- Mark temporary work as `TODO(name): description` so it is searchable. Stale docs are worse than no docs.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
// increment counter
export function check(d: Data) { return d.n > 0; }

// ✅ GOOD
/**
 * Returns whether a vehicle is due for service.
 * @param vehicle - Vehicle with last service date and interval days
 * @returns true when the service interval has elapsed
 */
export function isDueForService(vehicle: Vehicle): boolean {
  // Fleet policy uses calendar days, not business days — matches ops handbook §4.2
  return daysSince(vehicle.lastServicedAt) >= vehicle.serviceIntervalDays;
}
```
